%S A397898 -3,-7,0,-7,-11,-4,5,-15,-4,-11,-15,-8,1,12,-23,-12,1,-23,-8,-15,-19,
%T A397898 -12,-3,8,21,-31,-20,-7,8,-35,-20,-3,-31,-12,-19,-23,-16,-7,4,17,32,
%U A397898 -39,-28,-15,0,17,-47,-32,-15,4,-47,-28,-7,-39,-16,-23,-27,-20,-11,0,13,28,45
%N A397898 Values of the discriminant b^2 - 4*a*c of the quadratic equation a*x^2 + b*x + c = 0 for triples (a, b, c) of positive numbers, ordered first by their sum and then component-wise.
%C A397898 Let us clarify the ordering method for triples of natural numbers. First, triples with a smaller sum are taken, then those with a larger sum, and so on. Triples with equal sums are ordered component-wise: they are compared based on their first element; if these are equal, the second elements are compared; if those are also equal, the third elements are compared.
%F A397898 a(n) = A057557(3*n-1)^2 - 4*A057557(3*n-2)*A057557(3*n). - _Jason Yuen_, Aug 25 2026
%e A397898 a(1) = -3, because for the triple (1, 1, 1), the discriminant is 1^2 - 4*1*1 = -3;
%e A397898 a(2) = -7, because for the triple (1, 1, 2), the discriminant is 1^2 - 4*1*2 = -7;
%e A397898 a(3) = 0, because for the triple (1, 2, 1), the discriminant is 2^2 - 4*1*1 = 0;
%e A397898 a(4) = -7, because for the triple (2, 1, 1), the discriminant is 1^2 - 4*1*2 = -7.
%Y A397898 Cf. A057557, A379597.
